title: Go to definition on virtual property access (obj.ID) does not resolve to get_ID/set_ID
updated_at: "2026-08-31T12:57:54Z"
---
## Expected Behavior

Go-to-definition on the member name in a virtual property access resolves
to the accessor method(s) on the declaring class:

- `obj.ID` in a READ position → `uint get_ID()` on the class.
- `obj.ID = x` (write position) → `set_ID(...)` when it exists (else
  `get_ID` as fallback).
- Compound assignment (`obj.ID += 1`) reads AND writes → offer both
  accessors (LSP definition responses may be an array).

## Actual Behavior

Go-to-definition on `ID` in `obj.ID` resolves nothing (or falls through)
— `src/server/navigation.rs` has no `get_`/`set_` accessor mapping in its
definition path at all. The checker already understands the pairing
(ReadOnlyProperty diagnostics; B061 credits `get_X`/`set_X` usage from
property reads/writes), so the resolution logic exists in the workspace —
navigation just never consults it.

## Generalize (repo convention)

- Workspace class accessors (the motivating case).
- Namespace-level virtual properties: `Ns::Thing` backed by
  `Ns::get_Thing()` / `Ns::set_Thing()` (typedb has e.g.
  `Time::get_MilliStamp`; workspace namespaces can declare the same
  shape).
- Inherited accessors: `get_ID` declared on a base class, accessed via a
  derived-typed receiver.
- Interface-declared accessors.
- Negative control: a REAL field named `ID` must keep navigating to the
  field declaration, never to a same-named accessor elsewhere.
- Hover/signature-help on `obj.ID` may share the same resolution seam —
  check while in there, but the ticket's bar is definition.

## Notes

Read-vs-write position detection already exists for the checker's
ReadOnlyProperty / compound-assign handling — reuse that classification,
do not re-derive it in the server layer.

## Lane decision (2026-08-31, bl-fix-many batch B103-105)
Serialized with B104 in one worktree (.worktrees/nav): both tasks rewrite the goto-definition path in src/server/navigation.rs and its tests, so parallel lanes would conflict at merge. B103 lands FIRST (B104's generalize list includes an accessor-pair case that depends on B103's get_/set_ resolution existing).

## Landed (2026-08-31, merged with B104; master gate SUITE-VERIFIED 2802/0, 33/33)
Read/write/compound classification unified in src/typecheck/access_position.rs (checker + navigation share one rule). Accessor widening runs AFTER exact member lookup, so a real field always wins. Hover deliberately NOT wired to the accessor seam — follow-up filed as B113.